; The Windows target. NO PORTABILITY CLAIM, and less of one than any target so
; far: this emits x86-64 assembly and hands it to MASM.
;
; The surface is one file per "package", the same shape targets/go and
; targets/java have:
;
;   (use x64)                → (x64.add a b)  (x64.imul a b)  (x64.sar x 3)
;   (use windows/kernel32)   → (kernel32.WriteFile h p n)
;   (use windows/msvcrt)     → (msvcrt.printf1 fmt n)
;
; THE NAMES ARE THE HOST'S. On this host the host's names for arithmetic are
; INSTRUCTION MNEMONICS, so `+` is `x64.add` and `<` is `x64.setl`. That reads
; strangely and it is not a mistake: the rule was "keep the names as in the
; target", and assembly's name for adding is `add`.
;
; WHAT IS DIFFERENT ABOUT THIS TARGET. Every host before it was an expression
; language, so `expr "%s + %s"` could hand the operands to the host's own parser
; and let it rebuild the tree. Assembly has no tree. A template here is an
; INSTRUCTION SEQUENCE, and it needs three things no other target file needs:
;
;   %r     where to put the result. There is no expression to be the result.
;   %1..%9 operands by position, since instructions rarely use them in order.
;   %u     a unique number, so a template may carry its own labels — and
;          therefore its own loops. `x64.strlen` below is one.
;
; plus %b1/%br and %e1/%er, which spell an operand's register at 8 and 32 bits,
; because x86 gives one register three names.
;
; WHICH REGISTERS A TEMPLATE MAY TOUCH. rax, rcx, rdx, r8, r9 and xmm0-xmm3 are
; free. r10, r11, xmm4 and xmm5 are NOT: they carry spilled operands into the
; template. Neither are rbx, rsi, rdi, r12-r15 or xmm6-xmm13, which hold live
; values — and which is exactly why a Win32 call costs nothing here, since
; kernel32 preserves all of them.

(target windows
  ; THIS HOST SHIPS NO MAP, so the language supplies one -- `emit/winmap.oro`,
  ; open addressing over an ordinary `build` buffer, lowered before reduction.
  ;
  ; Declared rather than inferred, because there is nothing sound to infer it
  ; from: an empty `map-type` means "no map" here and "no TYPES" on JavaScript,
  ; which has a perfectly good map and spells nothing at all. Guessing would
  ; have handed JavaScript our hash table and thrown away a measured 3.67x.
  (builtin-map)

  ; THREE OF THESE NAMES ARE RESERVED BY THE LANGUAGE, not chosen here: `int`
  ; and `f64` are what integer and float LITERALS type as, and `bool` is what
  ; `if` and a loop guard demand. The right-hand side is the machine
  ; representation, which on this host is all a type can be.
  (type int    "qword")
  (type f64    "real8")
  (type bool   "qword")
  (type string "qword")   ; a pointer to NUL-terminated bytes
  (type ptr    "qword")
  (type any    "qword")

  ; STORAGE THE TARGET OWNS. This is new, and Win32 forced it. `WriteFile` takes
  ; a pointer to a cell it writes the byte count into, and our language has no
  ; pointers, no addresses and no way to spell an out-parameter. There was
  ; nowhere to put one — so the TARGET declares the cell and hides it inside the
  ; template, and the language never learns that it exists.
  ;
  ; __buf is the same trick for a different reason: printing a number needs a
  ; byte buffer, and the language cannot allocate one either.
  ;
  ; Each line is emitted ONLY IF its label appears in the code, the way an
  ; import is. 4096 bytes here made hello-win.exe 6656 against a hand-written
  ; 2560, for a buffer it never touches; with the check it is 2560 exactly.
  (data "__written qword 0")
  (data "__read    qword 0")
  (data "__qpc     qword 0")
  (data "__buf     db 256 dup(0)")

  (build "cmd /c .\\build.bat")
  (artifact "main.exe")

  ; THE STRUCTURAL SET IS ZERO. `if`, `let` and `loop` are the LANGUAGE's and are
  ; injected into every target; declaring one is an error. The backend implements
  ; them, which is where an implementation belongs — a construct promoted to the
  ; language works on every target and the compiler finds the form.
  ;
  ; and that is the result this target exists to test. `let`, `if` and `loop`
  ; are the only things the backend implements in code; every other name in
  ; this directory is a template.
)
